# ACMP SQL Server image = stock SQL Server 2022 + Full-Text Search (FR-143/145, AC-061).
# The stock mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server image ships WITHOUT Full-Text Search
# (SERVERPROPERTY('IsFullTextInstalled') = 0); global search needs it, so we install the
# mssql-server-fts package from the Ubuntu 22.04 Microsoft repo. The Arabic word-breaker
# (LCID 1025) rides in with it (OQ-034 spike, ph0-validation §7).
#
# ⚠⚠ ADR-0046 (2026-09-07): THIS IMAGE IS ON THE SQL SERVER 2022 LINE ON PURPOSE, AND DEPENDABOT IS
# TOLD NOT TO PROPOSE THE 2025 MAJOR (.github/dependabot.yml). It moved to 2025 on 2026-08-24 (#308)
# and from then on SearchProvidersFtsTests crashed at container start on ~3.5% of CI runs (DEF-121,
# DEF-130: SQLPAL faults in the LSA subsystem under CONCURRENT BOOT). SL-039/SL-040 isolated the base
# line as the variable one pair at a time — 2025 line 12/360 crashes vs 2022 line 0/840 under the same
# harness, and this exact content 0/480 with the real fixture path green 10/10 — with NO mechanism
# claimed. ADR-0046 d4 names the only thing that reopens it: a 2025 CU passing 360 starts at 0 under
# sql-startup-sampling.yml contention=heavy beside a same-day control that delivers. A crash of either
# family on THIS line is a NEW defect row, never an append.
#
# Base digest-pinned (C-SUP-01, P16b/B2b) — tag kept before @sha256: for Dependabot's CU (digest) bumps.
# 2022-latest == 2022-CU26-ubuntu-22.04 == sha256:ba4c8329… on 2026-09-07 (docker buildx imagetools inspect).
#
# ⚠⚠ THE `FROM` BELOW AND THE PACKAGE-REPO URL IN THE RUN BLOCK ARE ONE DECISION IN TWO
# PLACES, AND THEY MUST MOVE TOGETHER. The repo path encodes BOTH the Ubuntu release and the
# SQL Server major: ubuntu/<release>/mssql-server-<major>. Dependabot bumps only the FROM line
# and cannot see the coupling, so its bump PR can never go green on its own — which is exactly
# how #135 failed. mssql-server-fts pulls in mssql-server itself, so with a mismatched repo a
# mssql-server built for another Ubuntu release is installed OVER this base (the 22.04 build
# links liblber-2.5.so.0, which 24.04 does not ship). See DW-078 / PE-606.
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest@sha256:ba4c8329f48fb8f02e1416be6a930ebfd71268caee78aa985f3af4315e457c89
USER root
# ⚠⚠ DEF-140: THE FIRST LINE OF THE RUN BLOCK IS A FIX, NOT TIDYING — IT REMOVES THE ONLY THIRD-PARTY
# DEPENDENCY IN THIS BUILD THAT EVER STALLED IT, AND THE BUILD NEVER READ IT. On the 24.04 base the
# `universe` index from archive.ubuntu.com stalled a CI build for 5m31s until its 480-second budget
# (run 33939406843); trimmed to `main`, every index fetch completes in ~3 seconds — measured. The jammy
# (22.04) base lists its suites in /etc/apt/sources.list rather than ubuntu.sources, so here the
# universe/multiverse/backports lines are DROPPED rather than the Components edited; only `main` on
# jammy/jammy-updates/jammy-security is consulted. packages.microsoft.com was never the problem.
#
# ⛔ AND THIS IS WHY THE KEY IS NOT PIPED THROUGH gpg: gnupg2 lives in UNIVERSE, so keeping it would
# keep the very index that caused the stall. apt reads ASCII-armored keys straight from
# /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*.asc, so writing microsoft.asc there removes the only reason gnupg2 was
# ever installed. The two halves are ONE change; reverting either alone breaks the build.
#
# --no-install-recommends keeps the layer minimal (C-CON-002 / DS-0029) — no suggested extras.
#
# ⚠ DEF-157 (DEC-165 n3): THE APT LAYER IS RETRIED, AND THE RETRY IS FAIL-CLOSED. On 2026-09-10
# packages.microsoft.com served a corrupt InRelease ONCE ("Splitting up ... InRelease into data and signature
# failed" → "The repository ... is not signed"), and with no retry that single response killed the e2e job at
# 1m21s while the backend job built this same file green minutes earlier (run 34441522959). Three attempts,
# 20 s apart, with /var/lib/apt/lists wiped between them — a corrupt InRelease sits under lists/partial and
# would otherwise be re-read. Acquire::Retries and curl --retry cover the plain transport failures those tools
# retry themselves; the loop covers the VERIFICATION failure they do not.
# ⛔ A `for` loop that never `break`s still exits 0, so the `dpkg -s` line after it is the part that turns
# exhaustion into a red: without it an image WITHOUT full-text search builds, boots, passes its healthcheck,
# and fails the FREETEXT tests three layers later (trap 31: a gate with no subject must fail, not pass).
# ⚠ This reduces a probability rather than removing a condition (LL-035): it cannot be confirmed by absence,
# and DEC-132 d3 declined this shape for DEF-137's Docker Hub leg. DEC-165 n3 ordered it here. The removal
# route — build once, pull a pinned digest — is DEF-157's own upgrade path and is not taken in this change.
RUN sed -i -E '/universe|multiverse|backports/d' /etc/apt/sources.list \
 && for attempt in 1 2 3; do \
      rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
      && apt-get -o Acquire::Retries=3 update \
      && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl ca-certificates \
      && curl -fsSL --retry 3 https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc -o /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/microsoft.asc \
      && curl -fsSL --retry 3 https://packages.microsoft.com/config/ubuntu/22.04/mssql-server-2022.list -o /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mssql-server-2022.list \
      && apt-get -o Acquire::Retries=3 update \
      && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends mssql-server-fts \
      && break; \
      echo "DEF-157: apt layer attempt $attempt failed; retrying in 20 s" >&2; sleep 20; \
    done \
 && dpkg -s mssql-server-fts >/dev/null \
 && apt-get clean && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
USER mssql
# Docker-secrets shim (ADR-0032): if MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD_FILE is set, load the SA password from it; no-op otherwise
# (Testcontainers/dev pass the plain MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD env). INLINED, not a COPYed script, on purpose: this
# Dockerfile is built with TWO contexts — compose uses the repo root, SearchProvidersFtsTests uses deploy/ — so a
# COPY path cannot resolve in both. `exec` keeps sqlservr as PID 1 with signals intact.
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/sh", "-c", "if [ -n \"${MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD_FILE:-}\" ] && [ -f \"$MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD_FILE\" ]; then MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD=\"$(cat \"$MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD_FILE\")\"; export MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD; fi; exec /opt/mssql/bin/sqlservr"]
